    <definition xml:id="appendix-statistics-ch3-median-definition">
      <title>Median</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>median</term> of an ordered dataset separates the lower 50% and upper 50%. It may or may not be a data value and is often denoted <m>M</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="appendix-statistics-ch3-median-steps">
      <title>How do I calculate the Median?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>n</m> be sample size.</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Order data from smallest to largest.</li>
            <li><p>Check parity of <m>n</m>:
              <ul>
                <li>If <m>n</m> is odd, median is entry <m>\dfrac{n+1}{2}</m>.</li>
                <li>If <m>n</m> is even, median is average of entries <m>\dfrac{n}{2}</m> and <m>\dfrac{n}{2}+1</m>.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="appendix-statistics-ch3-median-example-1">
      <title>Example 1</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Weekly study hours for 7 students are shown below. Find the median by hand.
        </p>
